    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "I went through some renaming of package names, in particular python and python3 - as the convention seems to be to add a '3' to the end of the commands (I had submitted an issue to \"pip\" about them no longer using pip - and the reply was, paraphrased, that \"pip\" is python2 pip and pip3 is python3 pip.\r\n\r\nI have seen a few references to cython3 in posts - but when I pip3 install cython I end with \"cython\" with the following:\r\n\r\n    #!/opt/bin/python3.5\r\n    # EASY-INSTALL-ENTRY-SCRIPT: 'Cython==0.25.1','console_scripts','cython'\r\n    __requires__ = 'Cython==0.25.1'\r\n    import sys\r\n    from pkg_resources import load_entry_point\r\n    \r\n    if __name__ == '__main__':\r\n        sys.exit(\r\n            load_entry_point('Cython==0.25.1', 'console_scripts', 'cython')()\r\n        )\r\n\r\nWhich is fine - unless you have python2 and python3 installed side-by-side.\r\n\r\nI can resort to repackaging tricks (rename the file before building the package) - but pip3 install cython will still be broken.\r\n\r\nOr - is cython3 not an issue for you - only for packagers like myself (and others who want to work with python2 and python3 side-by-side)?",
