            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "On 19/11/2016 09:34, Robert Bradshaw wrote:\n\n> Generally, if you have both python2 and python3, it won't matter which one you use for the cython command (it runs the same in both Python 2 and Python 3, and the produced code is compatible with both).\n> \n> Sounds good - \"generally\" speaking. What might be an indication that \n> there is an \"exceptional\" situation?\n\nFor software packaging the issue is that two packages want to own the \nsame file - so the second package (generally) ends up owning it. If that \npackage gets removed, the file disappears.\n\nHowever, knowing this, and the names of both packages I can add some \ninstall logic to test for the other during install and uninstall - and, \nthinking out loud, add or remove a link to cython2 and/or cython3 as \n$prefix/bin/cython.\n\nThanks for the reply.\n\nHowever, this is still 'unresolved' for pip(3) install/uninstall.\n",
